<h1>The Import Pipeline</h1>
<p class="page-meta">What happens after a download completes.</p>

<h2>Automatic import</h2>

<p>
  When a download client reports a completed item, NGMS picks up the file path and runs it
  through the import pipeline automatically:
</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Parse</strong> — the filename is parsed to extract title, year, episode numbers, quality, and language</li>
  <li><strong>Match</strong> — the parsed title is matched against monitored series and movies in the database</li>
  <li><strong>Quality check</strong> — the detected quality is compared against the item's quality profile</li>
  <li><strong>Rename</strong> — the file is renamed according to your naming template</li>
  <li><strong>Move</strong> — file is moved (or hard-linked) to the correct root folder path</li>
  <li><strong>Notify</strong> — download complete notifications are dispatched</li>
  <li><strong>Plex scan</strong> — if Plex integration is enabled, the affected library section is scanned</li>
</ol>

<h2>Manual import</h2>

<p>
  Use the <strong>Import</strong> page to manually process files on disk that weren't
  downloaded via NGMS — ripped Blu-rays, pre-existing collections, or anything that fell
  outside the normal pipeline.
</p>

<div class="step">
  <div class="step-num">1</div>
  <div class="step-body">
    <h3>Import → Browse</h3>
    <p>Navigate to the directory containing the files. NGMS scans and groups files by their
    detected show or movie.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="step-num">2</div>
  <div class="step-body">
    <h3>Review matches</h3>
    <p>Each file shows its detected quality and the episode or movie it matched. If the match is
    wrong, click the title to search manually and pick the correct match.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="step">
  <div class="step-num">3</div>
  <div class="step-body">
    <h3>Import selected</h3>
    <p>Click <strong>Import</strong> to process. Files are renamed and moved. Any that fail
    (wrong quality, already imported, unmatchable) are left in place with an error message.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Hard links vs. copies</h2>

<table>
  <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>When used</th><th>Disk usage</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Hard link</strong></td>
      <td>Source and destination are on the same filesystem</td>
      <td>Zero extra space — both paths point to the same inode</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><strong>Copy</strong></td>
      <td>Source and destination are on different filesystems</td>
      <td>Double space during seeding/retention period</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="callout callout-tip">
  <span class="callout-icon">✦</span>
  <div class="callout-body">
    <strong>Recommended layout:</strong> Mount your downloads directory and your media root
    folders under the same bind mount or Docker volume. This ensures hard links are always
    available and you won't consume double disk space.
  </div>
</div>

<h2>Naming templates</h2>

<p>
  Rename patterns are configured in <strong>Settings → Media Management</strong>.
  Templates use token substitution:
</p>

<div class="code-block">
  <div class="code-block-header">
    <span class="code-dot code-dot-r"></span>
    <span class="code-dot code-dot-y"></span>
    <span class="code-dot code-dot-g"></span>
    <span class="code-block-title">Example series naming template</span>
  </div>
  <pre><span class="comment"># Folder</span>
<span class="string">&lbrace;Series Title&rbrace; (&lbrace;Year&rbrace;)</span>

<span class="comment"># Season folder</span>
<span class="string">Season &lbrace;season:00&rbrace;</span>

<span class="comment"># Episode file</span>
<span class="string">&lbrace;Series Title&rbrace; - S&lbrace;season:00&rbrace;E&lbrace;episode:00&rbrace; - &lbrace;Episode Title&rbrace; [&lbrace;Quality Full&rbrace;]</span>

<span class="comment"># Result:</span>
<span class="comment"># Breaking Bad (2008)/Season 01/Breaking Bad - S01E01 - Pilot [Bluray-1080p].mkv</span></pre>
</div>

